.TH RREP 1 "rrep @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.nh
.SH "NAME"
rrep \- replace patterns in files
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.B rrep
.RI [ OPTION ].\|.\|.
.I PATTERN
.I REPLACEMENT
.RI [ FILE ].\|.\|.
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
The \fBrrep\fR utility searches input
.IR FILE s
for matches to a given
.I PATTERN
and replaces those matches by a given
.IR REPLACEMENT .
The pattern is, by default, a basic regular expression.
The replacement string may contain special characters to refer to portions of
the matched pattern.
\fB&\fR refers to the portion of the line that matched
.IR PATTERN ,
and \fB\e1\fR through \fB\e9\fR refer to the corresponding matching
sub-expressions in
.IR PATTERN .
\fB\e\e\fR, \fB\e&\fR and \fB\en\fR insert a \fB\e\fR, \fB&\fR and newline,
respectively.
.PP
If no
.I FILE
is given, or if the only
.I FILE
is \fB\-\fR, standard input is read and the result is written to standard
output.
Binary files are, by default, ignored.
A file is regarded as binary if it contains the null character.
If processed, a null character in a binary file also ends a line.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
.BR \-E ", " \-\^\-extended\-regexp
Interpret
.I PATTERN
as an extended regular expression (ERE).
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\^\-fixed\-strings
Interpret
.I PATTERN
and
.I REPLACEMENT
as fixed strings, not as regular expressions or escape sequences.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\^\-recursive
Process each directory that is given on the command line recursively.
Files and sub-directories starting with the
.B .
character are, by default, ignored.
Symbolic links in these directories are skipped as well, except if given on the
command line.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\^\-dereference\-recursive
Likewise, but follow all symbolic links.
.TP
.BI \-\^\-include= FILE_PATTERN
Process only files that match the pattern
.IR FILE_PATTERN .
For conflicting \fB\-\^\-include\fR and \fB\-\^\-exclude\fR options, the last
matching option is used.
\fB*\fR, \fB?\fR, and \fB[\fR...\fB]\fR can be used as wildcards in
.IR FILE_PATTERN .
Wildcards and backslash characters can be quoted with \fB\e\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\^\-exclude= FILE_PATTERN
Skip files that match the pattern
.IR FILE_PATTERN ,
using wildcard matching as described under \fB\-\^\-include\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\^\-exclude\-dir= DIR_PATTERN
Skip directories that match
.IR DIR_PATTERN ,
using wildcard matching as described under \fB\-\^\-include\fR.
.TP
.BI \-S " SUFFIX" "\fR,\fP \-\^\-suffix=" SUFFIX
Override default backup suffix.
This option implicitly activates backups.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\^\-version
Print version information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\^\-all
Also process files and sub-directories starting with the \fB.\fR character in
recursively processed directories (see \fB\-\^\-recursive\fR).
.TP
.BR \-b
Back up before overwriting files.
The backup files are written into the directory of the original file.
Equivalent to \fB\-\^\-backup\fR without an argument.
.TP
.BI \-\^\-backup \fR[\fP = CONTROL \fR]\fP
Like \fB\-b\fR but accept a version control argument.
The backup suffix is appended to the file name.
For a simple backup, the suffix is \fB~\fR, unless set with \fB\-\^\-suffix\fR
or
.IR SIMPLE_BACKUP_SUFFIX .
For a numbered backup, the suffix is \fB.~\fR\fIN\fR\fB~\fR.
The version control method may be selected via the \fB\-\^\-backup\fR option or
through the
.I VERSION_CONTROL
environment variable, where \fBexisting\fR is the default.
Here are the values:
.RS
.PD 0
.TP 16
.BR none ", " off
never make backups (even if \fB\-\^\-backup\fR is given)
.TP
.BR numbered ", " t
make numbered backups
.TP
.BR existing ", " nil
numbered if numbered backups exist, simple otherwise
.TP
.BR simple ", " never
always make simple backups
.PD
.RE
.TP
.BR \-\^\-binary
Do not ignore binary files.
A file is regarded as binary if it contains the null character \fB\e0\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\^\-dry\-run
Simulate the replacement.
No file is actually modified.
.TP
.BI \-e " PATTERN" "\fR,\fP \-\^\-regexp=" PATTERN
Use
.I PATTERN
for matching.
This option can be used to specify a pattern beginning with \fB\-\fR.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\^\-help
Display a help message that describes the command line options and exit.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\^\-ignore\-case
Ignore case distinctions in
.IR PATTERN .
.TP
.BR \-\^\-interactive
Prompt the user each time before a file is modified.
The user can then cancel the modification.
.TP
.BR \-\^\-keep\-times
Restore the original access and modification times of files and directories
after processing.
.TP
.BI \-p " REPLACEMENT" "\fR,\fP \-\^\-replace\-with=" REPLACEMENT
Use
.I REPLACEMENT
for substitution.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\^\-quiet ", " \-\^\-silent
Suppress all normal output messages.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\^\-no\-messages
Suppress all error messages.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\^\-verbose
Print changed lines together with the corresponding original lines.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\^\-word\-regexp
Replace only those matches of
.I PATTERN
that match whole words.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\^\-line\-regexp
Replace only those matches of
.I PATTERN
that match whole lines.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
Exit status is 1 if any error occurs, 0 otherwise.
.SH "EXAMPLES"
.in +2n
.EX
$ \fBrrep\fP \(aqhello world\(aq \(aqHello, World!\(aq menu.h main.c
.EE
.in
.PP
This command replaces all occurrences of the string \(lqhello world\(rq by the
string \(lqHello, World!\(rq in the files menu.h and main.c.
.PP
.in +2n
.EX
$ \fBrrep\fP \-\^\-dry\-run \-\^\-verbose \(aqHello.*World.\(aq \
\(aqhello world\(aq menu.h main.c
.EE
.in
.PP
This command simulates the replacement of all occurrences of the pattern
\(lqHello.*World.\(rq like \(lqHello, World!\(rq by the string
\(lqhello world\(rq in the files menu.h and main.c and prints the changed
lines.
Due to the
.B \-\^\-dry\-run
option, the files are not actually modified.
